clean rebuild + all feature combos + exhaustive ###\n    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.12s\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 11 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.01s\ntest result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\ntest result: ok. 24 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.04s\n>>> PASS under (default)\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 11 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.01s\ntest result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\ntest result: ok. 24 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.04s\n>>> PASS under --no-default-features\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 11 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.01s\ntest result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\ntest result: ok. 24 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.05s\n>>> PASS under --all-features\n=========== nm -D symbol diff (C vs Rust) ===========\nsymbol diff EMPTY\n[EXHAUSTIVE] verified all 4294967296 u32 arguments identical\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 24 filtered out; finished in 25.47s\n=========== OVERALL: ALL GREEN ===========","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_blhslranhbwafabdlk3o64bgtcvbtiwu5oet2tjjhhyqcgkxwh7a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass from a clean rebuild.\n\n## What the library actually is\n\nThe C source is one function, nine lines, with **zero** branches, pointers, enums, allocations, or error returns:\n\n```c\nuint32_t rev16(uint32_t a) {\n    a = ((a & 0xAAAA) >> 1) | ((a & 0x5555) << 1);   // ... four 16-bit-masked swaps\n```\n\nEach mask is only 16 bits wide, so **statement 1 silently discards bits 16–31** and the result always lies in the low half. That's the classic trap here — the \"obvious fix\" is to widen the masks to 32 bits. The Rust reproduces the C verbatim and does not fix it, which is correct.\n\n## Results\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo check` | clean (no compile errors to fix) |\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | `nm -D` diff **empty** — C exports exactly `rev16`; Rust exports exactly `rev16`. 0 missing, 0 extra, 0 unresolved non-libc symbols |\n| Phase B — `CONFIGS.md` | 25/25 rows pass, 0 unchecked |\n| Phase C — `ERRORS.md` | 10/10 rows pass, 0 unchecked |\n| Feature combos | default / `--no-default-features` / `--all-features` all green (no `[features]` declared, 0 `cfg(feature)` in source) |\n| **Exhaustive** | **all 4,294,967,296 `u32` inputs byte-identical** through both `.so`s (~26s) |\n\nSince the entire input domain is a single `u32`, I exhausted it rather than sampling — this is a complete proof of equivalence, not a spot check. 39 tests total, all through `libloading`/`dlsym` on both shared objects; the Rust is never called directly, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is under test too.\n\n## Two findings worth flagging\n\n**1. My first mutation test was invalid, and I caught it.** To check the suite wasn't vacuous, I mutated the Rust and expected failures — but everything still passed. My initial instinct was to rationalize it as semantic equivalence. That was wrong: `str.replace(count=1)` had rewritten the **copy of the C code quoted in the doc comment** above `rev16`, not the code. The experiment had proven nothing. I rebuilt the harness to anchor on `^    <line>$` so it can only hit real code. Final mutation score: **4/4 behavior-changing mutations caught** (8–9 failing tests each), **2/2 behavior-preserving mutations correctly passed**.\n\n**2. I proved the two \"uncaught\" mutations were genuine equivalences rather than blind spots.** Widening statement 1's masks to 32 bits, and OR-ing in bit 16 on entry, both left the suite green. Rather than argue from reasoning, I brute-forced all 2³² inputs in C: **0 mismatches for both**. They really are equivalent (no bit crosses the bit-15/16 boundary under `>>1`/`<<1`, and bit 16 is discarded immediately). The suite was right to pass them.\n\n## Files written\n\n- `translation/{SYMBOLS,ERRORS,CONFIGS}.md` — Phase A artifacts, derived by mechanical grep (all recorded as **0 matches** for error/branch/pointer patterns)\n- `translation/tests/` — `common/mod.rs` (harness, seeded SplitMix64 PRNG), `valid_paths.rs` (25), `error_paths.rs` (11), `symbol_parity.rs` (4)\n- `translation/run_all_configs.sh` — one-command driver for all combos + symbol diff + exhaustive sweep\n- `translation/verification/` — mutation harness, exhaustive equivalence checker, and evidence README\n\n`c_src/` was not modified (only cmake's `build/` was added). Two notes on the environment: crates.io is blocked, so `--offline` is required (libloading 0.8.9 was in the local cache); and I added `[profile.dev] opt-level = 2` so the exhaustive sweep fits the timeout — it doesn't affect the release `.so` under test.\n\nOne judgment call to confirm: `ERRORS.md` has **no rows describing a rejection**, because the C rejects nothing — every `u32` is valid. I documented the empty error surface and repurposed the rows to assert the Rust also rejects nothing (no panic/abort/overflow trap) on null-pointer, zero-length, oversized, and out-of-range-enum *analogues*. Inventing rejection rows the C doesn't have would have been fabrication, but if you'd prefer that table framed differently, say so."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1061,"cache_read_input_tokens":103054,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1061,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8c0a516-4843-49bd-acd8-e3c3c600ce5b","uuid":"ed26c75b-5196-43bf-9795-b858328a0d0d","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:02:05.421Z"}
